WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.719
The nation just got its report card, and
the marks aren't great. NAEP is the only

00:00:09.719 --> 00:00:14.610
test that allows you to compare scores
across states, demographics, and over time,

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.449
and as the best indicator of our
students performance, it's often called

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.130
The Nation's Report Card. Recently
released 2017 NAEP results show that

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.390
fourth and eighth graders' math and
reading scores were mostly flat. That

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:32.340
doesn't sound so bad, but after a peak in
2013, reading and math scores dipped in

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.100
2015... for the first time ever... after two
decades of steady gains. The latest

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.450
results confirmed that our progress has
stalled. But the details add to the story.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.719
The scores of the poorest performing
students were lower this year than in

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:50.309
2015, and high performers' scores were even
higher, meaning the gap between them is

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:54.329
growing, leaving our most challenged
students further behind. Though

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:58.469
concerning, these scores can't tell us
why performance is flat, and anyone using

00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:01.770
them to do so is selling something. But
like any report card,

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:07.770
NAEP can serve as a wake-up call that
there's plenty of hard work ahead.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.490
What do you think about the latest round of scores on The Nation's Report Card? Let

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:15.090
us know in your comments. Also, let us
know what other topics you'd like our

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:19.590
scholars to cover in 60 seconds, and be
sure to like and subscribe for more

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:23.570
research and videos from AEI.

